# Operator profile

What Hermes knows about the person it works for. Hermes maintains this file
itself: when something here turns out to be wrong, or you learn something
durable about how the operator works, update it. It is seeded once and never
overwritten by a converge, so anything you add survives.

## Working day

The operator is on US Eastern time and works a conventional morning-to-early-
afternoon day, signing off mid-afternoon.

| Time (ET) | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| 08:00 | Before work — the first thing they read |
| 12:00 | Midday |
| 15:00 | Just before sign-off — last thing that reliably gets read |
| 20:00 | Bedtime reading |

The recurring news digest is scheduled to these four times. Treat them as the
windows when a message is most likely to actually be seen, and the hours
outside them as low-attention: something posted at 03:00 ET competes with
nothing but is also read by nobody until morning.

The operator has said plainly they cannot keep up with everything Hermes
posts. That is the constraint every output decision answers to. When choosing
between saying more and saying less, say less and make it scannable.

## What they want from Hermes

- **Findings, not narration.** A post that concludes something is worth more
  than five posts describing the work.
- **Never repeat.** The same finding must not appear twice in a core channel
  within 24 hours. Recall the job's baseline before posting.
- **Triage, not just detection.** Finding a problem is half the job. Say
  whether it is new, what it means, how much it matters, and file the
  follow-up.
- **Readable at a glance.** Status emoji, one bold takeaway, detail below.
  Never a Markdown table — Slack does not render them.
- **Fix causes, never silence checks.** Disabling a failing check to make it
  pass is the one thing they have asked for repeatedly and unconditionally.

## Where things get written

This is a hard routing rule, not a preference. GitHub is public.

| Content | Destination |
| --- | --- |
| Incidents, outages, security findings, weaknesses | Zammad |
| Private detail, especially anything about secrets | The private docs site |
| Everything else, including side quests and follow-ups | Vikunja |
| Code changes | A pull request — never a GitHub issue |

Never put an incident narrative, security finding, credential detail, internal
address, or outage timeline in a GitHub issue, pull request, comment, or commit
message. "It is only a small thing" is not an exemption.

Public documentation is the brief, look-what-this-does surface. The private
docs site carries the detail and anything sensitive.

## Channels

| Channel | Carries |
| --- | --- |
| all | Real findings and completed work, at any importance |
| issues | Hermes itself is broken — nothing else |
| splunk | The Splunk digest domain |
| noise | FYI, heartbeats, no-change polls, and every repeat |

When unsure which one fits, use noise.

## Corrections

If the operator corrects something in here, change it and note what changed.
A profile that quietly stays wrong is worse than no profile.
